/// Pitwall's tile API, version 0.1 (docs/spec/tile-sdk/runtime.md §3).
///
/// A tile is a WebAssembly component that Pitwall runs in a sandbox and
/// draws with its own UI code. The component describes what to show as a
/// tree of elements ([`types`]); Pitwall lays it out, draws it in its own
/// theme, and sends back what the user did as events. The component never
/// draws pixels and never sees Pitwall's process.
///
/// Version 0.x worlds may change between minor versions; Pitwall keeps the
/// last two 0.x worlds working (runtime.md §8.2). A component records the
/// world it was built for in its manifest's tile entry (`"world":
/// "pitwall:tile@0.1.0"`).
package pitwall:tile@0.1.0;

/// What a tile's component imports from Pitwall and exports to it.
///
/// Every import here works without a permission in 0.1: each one reaches
/// only the tile's own things (its tree, its log, its timers, its stored
/// data, its settings). Later versions add interfaces that need a
/// permission the user grants (network, project files, agents); a tile
/// that isn't granted one gets `host-error.denied` from it instead of
/// failing to load.
///
/// Besides these, the host provides WASI 0.2 for what Rust's standard
/// library needs: the wall and monotonic clocks (`std::time::SystemTime`
/// and `Instant` work), random numbers, and stdout and stderr, whose lines
/// go to the tile's Log (stdout as info, stderr as warnings). There are no
/// environment variables, arguments, files or sockets: keep data with
/// [`storage`]. In the test harness both clocks are virtual: they move
/// only when the test lets time pass.
world tile {
  /// Ask for renders, scroll a node into view, show a toast, open a view.
  import ui;
  /// Lines for the tile's Log.
  import log;
  /// Timers.
  import tasks;
  /// The tile's own key-value store.
  import storage;
  /// The tile's settings, as the user set them in Settings.
  import settings;

  /// What Pitwall calls in the tile.
  export guest;
}
